1. There was indeed a small error in the proof: we have added the missing factor 2w/(n-s). As you mentioned, it does not change anything in the result because it is indeed absorbed into f_1. We have also added the requirement that the parameters lie in N* rather than N, and the condition s, w < n to the lemma statement so that everything is clean. We have added the requested proof details in the paper about the fact that ((w-1)/(n-s-1), (u-1)/(n-s-1)) lies uniformly in the interior of A.

- Idea of the proof. Rather than relying on Taylor's theorem, we define f(w, u, n) = n/2 - sqrt(w*(n-w)) - u and prove that f(w-1, u-1, n-s-1) is in Omega(n-s) using elementary operations. We write f(w-1, u-1, n-s-1) = f(w, u, n-s) + D, where D = f(w-1, u-1, n-s-1) - f(w, u, n-s). A direct computation shows that D = 1/2 + sqrt(n-s-w) * (sqrt(w) - sqrt(w-1)) > 0, since both sqrt(n-s-w) and sqrt(w) - sqrt(w-1) are positive. We conclude using the parameter constraint Root(K_w^(n-s)) - u in Omega(n-s), which gives f(w, u, n-s) in Omega(n-s), and therefore f(w-1, u-1, n-s-1) in Omega(n-s).

