3. The proof details based on a Taylor argument will appear in the final version of the paper.

- Idea of the proof. We use the fact that w^{(j)} / n^{(j)} = w/n + O(1/n) and t^{(j)} / n^{(j)} = t/n + O(1/n) for all j in {1, ..., b}. Using the known formula for the gradient of the asymptotic expansion of Krawtchouk polynomials from [KS21, Section 2.1.2], we compute the gradient of the asymptotic expansion p of \delta_w^{(n-s)}(u). In our parameter regime, this gradient is bounded above in absolute value by a constant. Taylor's theorem then gives p(w^{(j)}/n^{(j)}, t^{(j)}/n^{(j)}) = p(w/n, t/n) + O(1/n) for each block j. Since b is a constant, summing over the blocks and exponentiating yields the result.

