4. Your remark is entirely valid. We would like to add that most code-based papers claiming asymptotic gains actually make this kind of reasoning, but it is kept completely implicit. We agree that for dual attacks the situation is more delicate than for ISDs due to wilder constraints, but in any case this was necessarily and crucially used in all of the original dual attacks [DT17, CDMT22, MT23, CDMT24]. Indeed, in those papers, the asymptotic parameters (\omega, \sigma, ..., real numbers in [0,1]) are optimized under an asymptotic variant of the constraint N > 1/delta^2. But this does not prove that there actually exist sequences of integers (w, s, ...) that achieve the non-asymptotic constraint and the claimed complexity: indeed there is some polynomial overhead between the asymptotic and non-asymptotic constraints. We agree that our paper is a good place to make this parameter-nudging argument fully explicit, so we have added a precise statement and proof in the paper.

- Detail on the statement. For any constant alpha > 0, and any parameters satisfying i) Root(K_w^{(n-s)}) - u in Omega(n-s) and ii) (n-s)/2 - w in Omega(n-s), it suffices to increase w by an additive term of at most O(log(n)) so that \binom{n-s}{w} \delta_w^{(n-s)}(u)^2 increases by a factor of at least Omega(n^alpha).

- Idea of the proof. The derivative of the asymptotic expansion of Krawtchouk polynomials is known from [KS21, Section 2.1.2]. Combining this with the known derivative of the binary entropy function, we easily compute the derivative of the asymptotic expansion of \binom{n-s}{w} \delta_w^{(n-s)}(u)^2 with respect to w. Using the first constraint, we easily show that this derivative is greater than log_2(sqrt((1 - w/(n-s)) / (w/(n-s)))). The second constraint ensures that this last term is positive and bounded away from 0. Taylor's theorem then allows us to conclude.

